  ; ⚠️ THE REST OF THE SHIFT FAMILY, which completes the integer half of
  ; A64_SIMD_3SAME. All six take a SIGNED amount from the low byte of each lane
  ; of `rm` -- left when it is positive, right when it is negative -- and they
  ; differ only in what happens at the two ends:
  ;
  ;            left overflows        right loses bits
  ;   SSHL     wraps                 truncates          (opcode 8, described)
  ;   SQSHL    saturates             truncates
  ;   SRSHL    wraps                 rounds
  ;   SQRSHL   saturates             rounds
  ;
  ; which is two independent choices, so the bodies below are built from two
  ; independent pieces rather than written out six times. That decomposition is
  ; only visible because a lane operator has a body to put it in.
  ;
  ; Three edges are worth naming because they are where a plausible description
  ; goes wrong. A left shift at or past the lane width cannot fit unless the
  ; lane was zero, so the saturating forms answer from the SIGN rather than
  ; from a product they cannot compute. An arithmetic right shift past the lane
  ; keeps the sign, where a logical one empties it. And a rounded right shift
  ; past the lane is zero, because the rounding constant alone already exceeds
  ; anything the lane can hold -- except at exactly the lane width unsigned,
  ; where it can carry to one, which is why the bound is `n < width + 1` and
  ; the arithmetic happens at twice the lane width.

